  "content": {
    "title": "BTS returns with emotional Netflix-streamed concert ahead of tour",
    "text": "BTS take the stage at Gwanghwamun Square in Seoul, South Korea, for Netflix’s “BTS the Comeback Live | Arirang,” which was livestreamed at 4 a.m. PST on Saturday, March 21.Big Hit Music and NetflixAfter an extended hiatus to complete South Korea’s mandatory military service, BTS group leader RM needed just five simple words to cut through the years of anticipation: “Hello, Seoul, we are back.”The Korean boy band’s first live show since 2022 was part of a promotional blitz coinciding with the Friday, March 20, release of their new full-length album “Arirang.” The comeback concert before more than 100,000 fans — and streamed live on Netflix at 4 a.m. Saturday, March 21, for Bay Area devotees — took place in Gwanghwamun Square in Seoul, in front of Gyeongbokgung Palace, a royal site dating to the Joseon dynasty. The setting in the urban capital’s center underscored the contrast between tradition and modernity that defines the city.Get Digital Access and Stay Informed With Trusted Local News.Get Digital Access and Stay Informed With Trusted Local News.ONLY 25¢RM performs in Seoul during the livestreamed “BTS the Comeback Live | Arirang.” He was seated due to an ankle injury incurred during rehearsals just days prior to the concert.Big Hit Music and NetflixThose expecting a comprehensive three-hour-long BTS revue were instead treated to a tight, one-hour, 12-song sampler of select “Arirang” tracks mixed in with a few of their well-known hits, including “Butter,” “Mic Drop” and “Dynamite.” Hardcore fans, known collectively as ARMY, might have groaned it was too short or didn’t include deep cuts. Newbies may have been annoyed that the between-song chatter, or “ments,” ate up too much time. Article continues below this adBut the concert run time and set list was never going to please everyone. The event was merely foreplay for BTS’ upcoming world tour, which pulls into Stanford Stadium for three days in May. With each member building impressive solo careers, the onstage return of BTS had the effect of a constellation — seven individual stars reunited as one. To witness rappers RM, J-Hope and Suga trade bars with one another, or singers V, Jimin, Jung Kook and Jin execute vocal lines within the same song together again was potent. It was emotional to see them locked into formation like Avengers reassembling. Audience members at “BTS the Comeback Live | Arirang” cheer during the Gwanghwamun Square concert in Seoul, South Korea, on Saturday, March 21.Big Hit Music and NetflixThe opening hip-hop salvo of “Body to Body,” “Hooligan” and “2.0” reflected their current “Arirang” state of mind, influenced as much by Los Angeles — where their new album was conceived — as their Korean heritage. As the members fanned across the stage hyping the crowd and themselves during “Body to Body,” the camera panned to a traditional group playing a brief snippet of the “Arirang” folk song to drive the East-meets-West point home.Article continues below this adSan Francisco Chronicle LogoMake us a Preferred Source to get more of our news when you search.Add Preferred SourceStyled in loose-fit outfits that reimagined traditional Korean dress and Joseon-era armor, the group balanced precision choreography with off-the-cuff interplay. But not everyone was dancing.RM was mostly seated for the show’s duration due to a severe left ankle sprain he incurred during rehearsals just two days prior. But the group made sure to keep engaged with their leader. During “Butter,” J-Hope went over and passed a freestyle pop-lock move to the seated RM, who took it and kept the cypher going. Cameras were able to adapt to the last-minute injury to keep RM present and involved.Netflix, who added the BTS concert to its burgeoning live event initiatives, also didn’t disappoint. The stream was glitch-free and the English subtitles, though understandably a little behind, were coherent enough for fans at home to follow along. And the show was available to rewatch in full immediately after it ended.BTS performs during “BTS the Comeback Live | Arirang,” their comeback concert after a four-year hiatus, at Gwanghwamun Square in Seoul, South Korea, on Saturday, March 21.Big Hit Music and NetflixThe comeback concert proved the familiar adage that distance makes the heart grow fonder. Article continues below this adAfter the crowd-pleasing “Dynamite,” BTS teased the event’s conclusion before adding one more song — the celebratory “Mikrokosmos” from 2019’s “Map of the Soul: Persona.” As lightsticks illuminated the city center like fireflies, BTS and ARMY serenaded each other and solidified their bond on a symbolic night held on sacred ground. “Perhaps the reason this night looks so beautiful,” the group sang in Korean, “is not because of these stars or lights, but us.”Todd Inoue is a freelance writer.Set list“Body to Body”Article continues below this ad“Hooligan”“2.0”“Butter”“Mic Drop”“Aliens”Article continues below this ad“FYA”“Swim”“Like Animals”Datebook Picks“Normal”“Dynamite”“Mikrokosmos”",
